// wire.mere — the versions of this API a running server will speak, and what
// happens to a client outside them.
//
// THE PROBLEM THIS ANSWERS (definition Q-4). admin.wasm is downloaded and
// cached by a browser, so a client built last week calls this week's server on
// every deploy. boundary.sh records which of those pairings work -- `v1 -> v2 :
// fail` is in boundary/EXPECTED right now. What it does not say is what the
// SERVER DOES about it, and the answer until this file was: nothing. The
// client sent no version, the server read none, and the failure happened
// entirely inside the browser's JSON decode. The server could not respond to
// it, log it, or count it.
//
// THE FIRST DEPLOY OF A VERSION HEADER IS THE ONE IT CANNOT PROTECT, and that
// is not a flaw in the design -- a build already sitting in a cache cannot be
// made to declare anything. Clients from this build forward say which wire
// they speak; the ones from before stay invisible, and are treated as they
// were before. The window is a promise about the future, and the honest
// version of that promise says so.
//
// WHY 409 AND NOT 426. RFC 2817's 426 Upgrade Required is defined for
// switching protocol on the same connection and carries an `Upgrade` header
// saying to what; there is nothing to switch to here. 409 Conflict is the
// accurate one: the request is well-formed and the caller is authorised, and
// what conflicts is the state of the caller's cached build with the state of
// the server. The body says so in a form a program can read.

let wire_current = 2;      // what this build's payloads are shaped like
let wire_min = 1;          // the oldest this server still serves
let wire_header = "X-Wire";

// An out-of-window answer has to be distinguishable from every ordinary
// failure, or a client that reloads on it reloads on ordinary failures too --
// which turns one bug into an infinite loop. The `action` field is what makes
// it distinguishable, and skew.sh asserts that a plain 4xx does not carry it.
// `\{` because a bare `{` starts string interpolation in Mere.
let wire_conflict_body =
  "\{\"error\":\"wire out of window\",\"min\":" ++ show wire_min
  ++ ",\"current\":" ++ show wire_current
  ++ ",\"action\":\"reload\"}\n";

// Nil means the caller declared nothing. That is not the same as declaring a
// bad version: an undeclared caller may be a build from before this header
// existed, a curl, or a monitor, and the server genuinely cannot tell which.
// It is served as it was before, and skew.sh asserts that too -- a hole that
// is written down and checked is different from one that is merely present.
// A DIGIT SCANNER RATHER THAN AN IMPORT. `parse_int_or_zero` lives in
// contrib/http/router, and importing it here put the whole router into
// admin.wasm -- 4KB of routing in a client that routes nothing. budget.sh
// caught it: wasm_admin 39024 -> 43133, over its ceiling. A header value is
// digits or it is not a version.
let rec _wire_digits = fn (s: str) -> fn (i: int) -> fn (acc: int) ->
  if i >= str_len s then acc
  else
    let c = substring s i (i + 1) in
    if str_eq c "0" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10)
    else if str_eq c "1" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 1)
    else if str_eq c "2" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 2)
    else if str_eq c "3" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 3)
    else if str_eq c "4" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 4)
    else if str_eq c "5" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 5)
    else if str_eq c "6" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 6)
    else if str_eq c "7" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 7)
    else if str_eq c "8" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 8)
    else if str_eq c "9" then _wire_digits s (i + 1) (acc * 10 + 9)
    else 0 - 1
  ;

let wire_declared = fn (raw: str) ->
  let t = str_trim raw in
  if str_eq t "" then None
  else let n = _wire_digits t 0 0 in if n < 0 then Some (0 - 1) else Some n
  ;

let wire_in_window = fn (v: int) -> v >= wire_min && v <= wire_current;
